/**
 * @file spl.dox
 * @brief Repository SPL DSL overview.
 *
 * @page spl_parser SPL DSL Overview
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section spl_purpose Purpose
 *
 * The SPL module parses the repository DSL, validates its structural rules,
 * compiles theory formulas to an executable form, evaluates those formulas on
 * concrete system IDs, and derives the `system_space_t` consumed by feature
 * analysis and isolation.
 *
 * This page is the API-and-semantics overview. For user-facing authoring
 * guidance, start with @ref spl_authoring_guide. For canonical fixtures and
 * common pitfalls, see @ref spl_examples_guide.

 *
 * @section spl_start_here Start here
 *
 * Recommended reading order:
 *
 * - @ref spl_authoring_guide
 * - @ref spl_examples_guide
 * - @ref spl_parser
 *
 * @section spl_top_level Top-level DSL structure
 *
 * The accepted DSL contains these sections:
 *
 * - `SPL <name>`
 * - `FEATURES <count> <feature...>`
 * - optional `CONSTRAINTS ... ASSERT <formula> ... END`
 * - optional `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS ... END`
 * - optional `MISSING_SYSTEMS ... END`
 * - optional `CATEGORY_FILTER ...`
 *
 * `FEATURES` is required. The remaining sections are optional and are parsed
 * into `spl_specification_t`.
 *
 * @section spl_formulas Supported formula language
 *
 * The formula language supports:
 *
 * - feature references,
 * - `TRUE` and `FALSE`,
 * - unary negation `!`,
 * - conjunction `&&`,
 * - disjunction `||`,
 * - implication `->`,
 * - equivalence `<->`,
 * - `COUNT(...)` comparison constraints such as `<=`, `>=`, `==`, `!=`, `<`,
 *   and `>`,
 * - `COUNT(...) IN [lower, upper]` interval constraints.
 *
 * The parser stores formulas in `theory_formula_t` with nested node types such
 * as `feature_ref_t`, `negation_t`, `conjunction_t`, `count_compare_t`, and
 * `count_interval_t`.
 *
 * `COUNT(...)` is repository syntax for expressing the paper's cardinality
 * idea. The paper itself presents equivalent constraints arithmetically rather
 * than with a literal `COUNT(...)` token.
 *
 * @section spl_execution Execution semantics
 *
 * The parser compiles feature references to numeric indices, after which
 * `system_satisfies_constraints` evaluates the conjunction of asserted formulas
 * for each candidate system ID. From that executable semantics the module
 * derives:
 *
 * - `valid_system_ids(spec)`
 * - `available_valid_system_ids(spec)`
 * - `missing_valid_system_ids(spec)`
 * - `theory_system_space(spec)`
 *
 * Availability blocks are interpreted only over valid systems. If exactly one
 * of `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS` or `MISSING_SYSTEMS` is given, the other is derived by
 * complement within the valid system space.
 *
 * @section spl_reporting Reporting-related fields
 *
 * `CATEGORY_FILTER` is parsed into `spl_specification_t::m_category_filter`.
 * It is a reporting hint only: it does not alter formula evaluation, validity,
 * or derived-feature construction.
 *
 * `theory_dsl_coverage` summarizes which DSL capabilities of a specification
 * are used and which paper-level notions remain outside the active parser.
 *
 * @section spl_examples Canonical examples
 *
 * Canonical valid fixtures used by the repository include:
 *
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/unconstrained_2f.spl`
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/fx_implies_fy.spl`
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/fx_implies_fy_missing_s0.spl`
 * - `spl_specs/new_dsl/valid/factorial_paper.spl`
 *
 * See @ref spl_examples_guide for walkthroughs and common invalid patterns.
 *
 * @section spl_boundaries Module boundaries
 *
 * This module owns parsing and executable constraint semantics. It does not
 * classify derived features and it does not compute isolation witnesses.
 * Those responsibilities belong to the feature-analysis and isolation modules.
 */
